From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SushyDev <mail@sushy.dev>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 23:09:04 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dp: validate modes against the trained lane count

link_info.num_lanes is the smaller of the sink's advertised maximum and
the data-lanes property in DT.  Both are static, so on a USB-C port it
describes the wiring rather than the link: DisplayPort alt mode decides
at attach time how many lanes are available, and a pin assignment that
keeps USB 3 leaves only two of the four for DP.

Sizing the link from that static value means a two lane link advertises
modes needing four lanes' worth of bandwidth.  Link training later falls
back to the lane count that is really there, but the mode has already
been accepted, so the user gets a black screen from a mode the driver
offered.

On a Samsung Odyssey G85SB in its USB data mode, DP trains at two lanes
and 3440x1440@120 (16.77 Gbps) was offered against 8.64 Gbps.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_display.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_display.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_display.c
index 46a5ce1..d11c502 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_display.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_display.c
@@ -697,6 +697,7 @@ enum drm_mode_status msm_dp_bridge_mode_valid(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
 	struct msm_dp_display_private *msm_dp_display;
 	struct msm_dp_link_info *link_info;
 	u32 mode_rate_khz = 0, supported_rate_khz = 0, mode_bpp = 0;
+	u32 num_lanes;
 	struct msm_dp *dp;
 	int mode_pclk_khz = mode->clock;
 	int mode_pclk_orig = mode->clock;
@@ -736,7 +737,11 @@ enum drm_mode_status msm_dp_bridge_mode_valid(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
 					mode_bpp, link_pclk_khz);
 
 	mode_rate_khz = link_pclk_khz * mode_bpp;
-	supported_rate_khz = link_info->num_lanes * link_info->rate * 8;
+	num_lanes = msm_dp_display->link->link_params.num_lanes;
+	if (!num_lanes)
+		num_lanes = link_info->num_lanes;
+
+	supported_rate_khz = num_lanes * link_info->rate * 8;
 
 	if (mode_rate_khz > supported_rate_khz)
 		return MODE_BAD;
